\begin_layout Standard
The main steps of the algorithm that produces classification programs are listed subsequently.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Initialization step
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Initialize
\series default
 the Genetic Algorithm parameters:
 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c}$
\end_inset

,
 the population size (number of chromosomes);
 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{g}$
\end_inset

,
 the maximum number of generations;
 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{s}$
\end_inset

,
 the selection rate;
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{m}$
\end_inset

,
 the mutation rate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Generate 
\series default
the initial population of 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c}$
\end_inset

 chromosomes,
 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{i},\;i=1,\ldots,N_{c}$
\end_inset

,
 where each chromosome consists of a sequence of randomly generated integer-valued codons.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Set
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $k=0$
\end_inset

,
 the generation counter.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Fitness calculation step
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
For
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N_{c}$
\end_inset

 
\series bold
do
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Map
\series default
 each chromosome 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{i}$
\end_inset

 to its corresponding classification program
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}$
\end_inset

 through the genotype-to-phenotype mapping process defined by the BNF grammar presented in Algorithm 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
ref{alg:genclass_bnf}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Evaluate
\series default
 the generated classification program 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}$
\end_inset

 on the training set and compute its classification error.
 The resulting error value,
 denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 is used as the fitness value of the corresponding chromosome 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
End For
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Application of genetic operations
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Selection
\series default
:
 Rank the chromosomes according to their fitness values in ascending order.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-p_{s})N_{c}$
\end_inset

 chromosomes with the lowest fitness values are directly transferred to the next generation without modification,
 while the remaining 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{s}N_{c}$
\end_inset

 chromosomes are replaced by new offspring generated through the crossover and mutation operators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Crossover
\series default
:
 Generate pairs of offspring by applying the one-point crossover operator.
 For each pair of offspring,
 denoted as 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 two parent chromosomes,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 are selected from the current population using tournament selection.
 A crossover point is then randomly selected along the chromosomes,
 and the genetic material located after this point is exchanged between the two parents.
 Consequently,
 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{1}$
\end_inset

 inherits the first part of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{1}$
\end_inset

 and the second part of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 whereas 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{2}$
\end_inset

 inherits the first part of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{2}$
\end_inset

 and the second part of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 A graphical illustration of the one-point crossover operation is provided in Figure 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
ref{fig:onePoint}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Mutation
\series default
:
 For each gene
\begin_inset Formula $g_{i,j}$
\end_inset

 of chromosome 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 a random number 
\begin_inset Formula $r\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

 is generated.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $r\leq p_{m}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{m}$
\end_inset

 denotes the mutation rate,
 the corresponding gene is replaced by a randomly generated integer value within the predefined range of admissible codon values.
 Otherwise,
 the gene remains unchanged.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Termination check step
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Set
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $k=k+1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
If
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $k\le N_{g}$
\end_inset

 
\series bold
then go to
\series default
 Fitness Calculation step 
\series bold
else
\series default
 terminate.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
